 First, we need to analyze the inequality \(\frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1} < \frac{1}{10}\). The left side of the inequality can be simplified as follows: \[
\frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n+1} = \frac{(n+1) - n}{n(n+1)} = \frac{1}{n(n+1)}
\] Thus, the inequality becomes \(\frac{1}{n(n+1)} < \frac{1}{10}\). This inequality is equivalent to \(n(n+1) > 10\). We need to find the smallest positive integer \(n\) such that this inequality holds. We can start testing small values of \(n\). For \(n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5, \ldots\), we can compute \(n(n+1)\) and check when it becomes greater than 10. \[
\begin{align*}
n = 1 & \implies n(n+1) = 1 \cdot 2 = 2 \\
n = 2 & \implies n(n+1) = 2 \cdot 3 = 6 \\
n = 3 & \implies n(n+1) = 3 \cdot 4 = 12 \\
\end{align*}
\] We see that for \(n=3\), \(n(n+1) = 12 > 10\), so the smallest positive integer \(n\) that satisfies the inequality is \(3\). </think> <answer> 3 </answer>

